{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce","id":115776,"title":"Godly Marriage and Divorce","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-04-19T04:16:42-07:00","id":115776,"title":"Godly Marriage and Divorce","content":"Me and my playgroup were joking about godlymarriage/divorce and we laughed a lot, then i had the idea what woul happen in scion:<br /> <br /> 1) Two gods from the same/different pantheon/s are married, do one of the gods become member of the other pantheon or both become honorary member of the so?<br /> <br /> 2) One of the gods dies, what happens to his belongins sons/daughters etc?<br /> <br /> 3) the god who died comes back from the dead, is the marriage still intact or not(... till death do us apart)<br /> <br /> 4) what could be cause of godly divorce? what WOULD happen in a divorce aside pettines and political fallout?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2768-zorker","name":"Zorker","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T04:38:03-07:00","id":115782,"title":"","content":"Interesting question. Personally, I'm of the view that as someones Legend grows (ie, they become gods), they become constrained by said Legend. Ares can't back down from a fight, because his Legend binds his behaviour as much or more as it warps the world around him.<br /> <br /> So, in this scenario, they wouldn't marry a god from another pantheon, because they wouldn't...resonate? like that. And if they did, I suspect their marriage would carry a lot more metaphysical weight, so they wouldn't get a divorce. Hera and Zeus are still married, despite their deep antipathy: the marriage becomes part of their Legend, and this cannot be removed simply.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T04:42:53-07:00","id":115784,"title":"","content":"1. Over at <a href=\"http://www.gothambynight.com/scion/index.htm\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.gothambynight.com/scion/index.htm</a> they have an Aesir and Aztec good that got married and an extensive fiction section that might explain it. It looks like they are listed as honorary members with no actual powers within the Pantheon.<br /> <br /> 2. Really depends on the individuals I would think. Just hope the surviving one doesn't have Vengeance as a virtue.<br /> <br /> 3. I don't think dead was an issue for Osiris' marriage.<br /> <br /> 4. I am not aware of any divine divorces. There are however some very distant relationships where the gods go about taking whatever lover they please. What would actually happen, I would imagine a few apocalyptic battles may be in order. Depending on how well liked the gods are in their home Pantheon it could even mean inter-pantheon war.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/151-hark","name":"Hark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar151_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"932"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T06:08:53-07:00","id":115810,"title":"","content":"I don't see why Gods of different Patheons might not marry. We have no such example in mythology, at least none I'm aware of, but that doesn't mean it can't happen in game, and certainly it can happen among player characters. In any case, how they are viewed by the two Pantheons depends a lot on the Virtues, and the circumstances of the marriage, and with how each Pantheon views each of the married couple.<br /> <br /> If they have kids when they are Gods, i'd imagine they would decide to which Pantheon they belong to, and that would be it. If they have Scions (by doing it in Avatar form), then their Scions will belong to whichever Pantheon activates them first, which will likely involve lots of political maneuvering.<br /> <br /> Death isn't an issue to all gods, so I don't think their marriages are &quot;Till death do us apart&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/721-greyskull","name":"Greyskull","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"127"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T06:33:23-07:00","id":115816,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Allan53</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=115782#post115782\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interesting question. Personally, I'm of the view that as someones Legend grows (ie, they become gods), they become constrained by said Legend. Ares can't back down from a fight, because his Legend binds his behaviour as much or more as it warps the world around him.<br /> <br /> So, in this scenario, they wouldn't marry a god from another pantheon, because they wouldn't...resonate? like that. And if they did, I suspect their marriage would carry a lot more metaphysical weight, so they wouldn't get a divorce. Hera and Zeus are still married, despite their deep antipathy: the marriage becomes part of their Legend, and this cannot be removed simply.</div> </div> </div> </div>Perhaps not such a good example with Ares, or many of the Greek pantheon. For 2e, the same god can be both the Greek version and the Roman version, even if the two versions are different - see Zeus and Jupiter. Gods can be... bigger and more multi-faceted than how they're seen in a particular pantheon, even given that how they're seen _in_ a particular pantheon could be pretty expansive (see the Romans).<br /> <br /> I'd say inter-pantheon marriages could be a thing. Whether either would become a member of the other's pantheon would probably be dependent on a number of factors - some part personal choice, some part general pantheon opinion, some part mortal view, etc.<br /> <br /> EDIT:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Greyskull</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=115810#post115810\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If they have kids when they are Gods, i'd imagine they would decide to which Pantheon they belong to, and that would be it. If they have Scions (by doing it in Avatar form), then their Scions will belong to whichever Pantheon activates them first, which will likely involve lots of political maneuvering.</div> </div> </div> </div>Multi-pantheon membership <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/81399-what-will-you-pantheons-be-like?p=85369#post85369\">has been mentioned by the 2e writers</a> (Mithras has a claim to a place in both the Roman and Persian pantheons, Guanyin to both China and Japan). They wouldn't necessarily have to choose one or the other.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":51,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","user":"marin","date":". 04-19-2014, 09:47 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T06:52:53-07:00","id":115822,"title":"","content":"<br /> 1) Two gods from the same/different pantheon/s are married, do one of the gods become member of the other pantheon or both become honorary member of the so?<br /> 1) What we know about the actual Gods is that mortals keep them &quot;alive&quot; by still believing in their existances. If two Gods happened to marry each other, every mortal would have to acknowledge it to make it happen. The book states that Gods are made of mortals' expectactions which is legit since mythology was invented by Man. But everything is linked to Fate. In order for two Gods to be married, mortals are the only one in charge and I'm not sure that collective unconscious is powerful enough to mix any more ancient Gods together.<br /> Without marriage, there are still gods and goddesses that appear in different mythology, especially the Wicca. Some Gods are borrowed and that's because it was wanted. Artemis might belong to the Dodekatheon, but if we take into account that the Wicca still exists, she might just wanna be Wiccan instead of Greek. If being known means to exist, two married Gods should join the pantheon that is most likely to be the strongest. I hope I was clear enough...<br /> <br /> 2) I don't really get the &quot;what happens&quot;. If you're talking about heritage, I don't think it works that way. In order to become god, you have to build your Legend so they won't take his place until he fulfills his part of the deal or... Fate makes him God.<br /> <br /> 3) Technically, the bond was broken when the God died. And so the marriage. Coming back to live means he died once. But that is only available for Christians. Ancient Gods may be tied by stronger bonds (fatebinding) and marriage may be the ultimate binding.<br /> <br /> 4) Since no God ever got divorce, it's hard to tell. Still, I don't think it can happen because marriage is a sacred vow that cannot be broken. That's why Hera gets revenge on Zeus' offsprings or his lovers. And again, divorce is a too new concept for Ancient Gods.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1837-wickedgrail","name":"Wickedgrail","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1837_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T07:07:51-07:00","id":115827,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wickedgrail</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=115822#post115822\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) Two gods from the same/different pantheon/s are married, do one of the gods become member of the other pantheon or both become honorary member of the so?<br /> 1) What we know about the actual Gods is that mortals keep them &quot;alive&quot; by still believing in their existances. If two Gods happened to marry each other, every mortal would have to acknowledge it to make it happen. The book states that Gods are made of mortals' expectactions which is legit since mythology was invented by Man. But everything is linked to Fate. In order for two Gods to be married, mortals are the only one in charge and I'm not sure that collective unconscious is powerful enough to mix any more ancient Gods together.<br /> Without marriage, there are still gods and goddesses that appear in different mythology, especially the Wicca. Some Gods are borrowed and that's because it was wanted. Artemis might belong to the Dodekatheon, but if we take into account that the Wicca still exists, she might just wanna be Wiccan instead of Greek. If being known means to exist, two married Gods should join the pantheon that is most likely to be the strongest. I hope I was clear enough...</div> </div> </div> </div>...Er. That seems to be going _far_ further towards the &quot;gods are dependent on human belief&quot; position than Scion actually does, particularly in saying human belief is what keeps them alive. Could I get references for that?<br /> <br /> (Also, God p. 155 says the earliest gods were born of the Titans. Human expectations have shaped the gods, but it's not where they began.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T07:19:40-07:00","id":115833,"title":"","content":"Scion: hero p.220 <br /> &quot;Fate influences all, from Gods to mortals to even Titans. Understanding its manipulations is vital to predicting its ordained results. Where does one begin? With the measure of all things: Man. While the Gods do not fully understand Fate, they know that it is somehow tied to mortals. Before they began interfering in the lives of these beings of clay, the Gods were relatively free of Fate\u2019s invisible hand, but now, their very essences are entwined with mortal expectations. Their forms rely on mortal beliefs to keep them coherent and free from merger with their Titan parents. What is this amazing power that mortals have, of which they are entirely unaware? In short, it\u2019s Story. The human mind craves stories.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1837-wickedgrail","name":"Wickedgrail","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1837_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T09:58:39-07:00","id":115929,"title":"","content":"Divorce, as a concept, is just as much a divisive issue WITHIN pantheons, as a lot of those marriages are the platonic models for the concept of marriage within that ancient culture. <br /> <br /> What if Hera figured out a way to divorce Zeus and still stay within her own baliwick? She's always hated all the affairs. She's got Ulitmate Manipulation and she's had 1000's of years to manipulate any culture-sized Fatebonds... What would happen to the Dodekatheon if &quot;mom and dad&quot; were living in separate palaces... You know everyone would be forced to choose sides. How would that affect their ability to fight the Titanomachy (Redux)?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T10:41:54-07:00","id":115941,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wickedgrail</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=115833#post115833\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Scion: hero p.220<br /> &quot;Fate influences all, from Gods to mortals to even Titans. Understanding its manipulations is vital to predicting its ordained results. Where does one begin? With the measure of all things: Man. While the Gods do not fully understand Fate, they know that it is somehow tied to mortals. Before they began interfering in the lives of these beings of clay, the Gods were relatively free of Fate\u2019s invisible hand, but now, their very essences are entwined with mortal expectations. Their forms rely on mortal beliefs to keep them coherent and free from merger with their Titan parents. What is this amazing power that mortals have, of which they are entirely unaware? In short, it\u2019s Story. The human mind craves stories.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>You're reading that wrong. It's basically saying that the Gods exist in their present state because Mortal belief shaped them to be that way and through fate forces them to conform to ortal expectations of them. The Gods would probably be much happier and definitely have far more free will if they had never been involved with mortals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/151-hark","name":"Hark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar151_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"932"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T12:40:19-07:00","id":116042,"title":"","content":"I understood what you said and that's what I thought I understood too. I apologize if I made it sounds otherwise.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1837-wickedgrail","name":"Wickedgrail","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1837_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-19T15:02:57-07:00","id":116135,"title":"","content":"Went back and re-read what you originally said, thought you were saying gods exist because of belief and supporting that with the quote.<br /> <br /> Fate certainly bind Gods to patterns and set traits, but nothing stops them from doing anything unless it directly conflicts with established Legend. In the case of Marriage, nothing stops them from getting married unless a critical aspect of their Legend is that the god be unmarried. A God may even me able to actively cultivate a Legend promoting development of a relationship toward Marriage by actively going on dates in full view of mortals. Divorce may even be accomplished in the same manner by feuding with your spouse in front of mortals and threatening Divorce.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/151-hark","name":"Hark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar151_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"932"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T00:09:25-07:00","id":116384,"title":"","content":"I think we can agree being divorced directly conflicts with being married, and if their Legend incorporates that, then by your own argument it'd be restricted.<br /> <br /> Gods are as much concepts as they are beings, as I understand them, in Scion. They may have been entities before, but now they're largely powered by Legend, which is where they derive the vast bulk of their power and identity. So I don't know if they <i>could</i> go against that, even if they wanted to.<br /> <br /> Sure they could get married, unless their Legend says that they're single in a significant extent (like, they're partially defined that way, not just 'oh, and he/she happens to be single'). Course, 'married' and 'monogamous' aren't remotely the same thing: it's very possible that Zeus can't stop screwing around any more than he can stop throwing lightning bolts or whatever. It's part of his Legend, what he's known for.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T06:17:13-07:00","id":116469,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zorker</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce#post115776\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">4) what could be cause of godly divorce? what WOULD happen in a divorce aside pettines and political fallout?</div> </div> </div> </div>While the other questions have been adequately answered from a variety of viewpoints, I'd like to point out that there is a case in mythology of a semi-divorce, in a sense. Now, obviously, divorce in our modern context is not something we find much of in mythology, there is a case where one God basically packed her bags and left with all intention to end the marriage, and that God happens to be Hera. Hera, tired of Zeus' constant philandering, left Olympus, and Zeus was devastated because contrary to popular belief, and contrary to our modern sensibilities, Zeus is very much in love with Hera. He moved heaven and earth to get her back, and when she refused to surface he resorted to announcing that he was remarrying. Hera heard this and instantly forgot her anger in her jealousy (because Hera is also, contrary to popular belief, in love with Zeus), and rushed to the wedding to find that the 'bride' was a wooden doll and Zeus was ready to remarry her.<br /> <br /> I would also like to point out that Hera is technically not the Goddess of Marriage (That's Hymaeneus' job). She's the Goddess of Marital Fidelity, and hence can totally divorce Zeus without it interfering with her role.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T07:24:39-07:00","id":116492,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=116469#post116469\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I would also like to point out that Hera is technically not the Goddess of Marriage (That's Hymaeneus' job). She's the Goddess of Marital Fidelity, and hence can totally divorce Zeus without it interfering with her role.</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">If she's really about marital fidelity, she would have already divorced because Zeus broke one of the vows. You could say that fidelity is not about breaking up if your husband cheats on you. True, but if Hera represents Marital fidelity, she can't think of divorce because one of the vow says &quot;<span style=\"color:#333333\">With unfailing love I will stand by you, through the best and the worst, through the difficult and the easy.&quot; And she shouldn't have left. Maybe Hera isn't the best example. Let's talk about Persephone. She's &quot;cursed&quot; (for Demeter) to be married to Hades eternally for eating something in the Underworld. Fate wouldn't let her go even if she wanted to.<br /> <br /> It seems &quot;funny&quot; to see how mythology thinks about marriage when in most of political circumstances, it has always been... political. (yeah, that's besides the point)</span></span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1837-wickedgrail","name":"Wickedgrail","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1837_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T07:54:31-07:00","id":116498,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wickedgrail</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=116492#post116492\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">With unfailing love I will stand by you, through the best and the worst, through the difficult and the easy.&quot; </span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">Is that what an Ancient Greek Wedding vow says?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wickedgrail</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=116492#post116492\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Let's talk about Persephone. She's &quot;cursed&quot; (for Demeter) to be married to Hades eternally for eating something in the Underworld. Fate wouldn't let her go even if she wanted to.</div> </div> </div> </div>We have different ideas of how Fate works in Scion (I was never too fond of the RAW version), so yes, by your reading it would probably take a Purview Avatar for Persephone to get out of Hades.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wickedgrail</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=116492#post116492\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems &quot;funny&quot; to see how mythology thinks about marriage when in most of political circumstances, it has always been... political. (yeah, that's besides the point)</div> </div> </div> </div></span></span><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wickedgrail</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=116492#post116492\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"></div> </div> </div> </div>There are a few stories of true love in myth. Aengus and Caer Ibormaeith of the Tuatha, the Trimurti and Tridevi for the Deva (especially Shiva and Parvati), Hi'iaka and Hopoe of the Polynesian gods come to mind. There's also Odysseus and Penelope.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1344,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","user":"Samudra","date":". 04-20-2014, 10:56 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T08:16:46-07:00","id":116505,"title":"","content":"I'm thinking in Ancient Greek terms, legally, a divorce is a little different from separation. Running off and leaving Zeus doesn't make him less her husband, it simply deprives him of her presence. Divorce is ending the marriage permanently. I'd have to dig through some books to look it up exactly and, obviously, there are a ton of different legal systems that would come into play - by era, city, region, culture, etc. The laws of Sparta regarding marriage and the laws of Periclean Athens won't match up and both will be very different from the Late Roman Empire.<br /> <br /> If Hera wanted to get divorced, she would be so bound by her Fate that she couldn't use her own power to affect that? Not even Ultimate Manipulation? Even after spending 1500 years + staying away from mortals so she wouldn't have to be completely bound by their culture-wide Fatebonds?<br /> <br /> If so, that sucks for PCs who get to be gods. I would probably get a little rebellious in the game if I burned all that Legend to be an Avatar or for an Ultimate Attribute and found out that I was precluded because of what some mortals thought of me. Sure, it might be HARDER because of the weight of that mass of belief influencing Fate, but if I'm clever or persistent or whatever, shouldn't I have a chance?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T08:38:09-07:00","id":116518,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=116498#post116498\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Is that what an Ancient Greek Wedding vow says?</div> </div> </div> </div>You meant, is that what ancient greek mythology wedding vow says. That's actually my point: there's no way to find out if divorce was plausible at all. It was a good question but there is no answer unless we know how marriage works back up there. And there's no way to find out. However, I think that divorce was never something they would have do either way because I've never heard of Gods divorcing. I know the question was if it were possible but it can't be.<br /> <br /> Now, in Scion, Scion has free will. The question is, does she keep it when she becomes a god ?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1837-wickedgrail","name":"Wickedgrail","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1837_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T11:00:46-07:00","id":116583,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=116505#post116505\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If so, that sucks for PCs who get to be gods. I would probably get a little rebellious in the game if I burned all that Legend to be an Avatar or for an Ultimate Attribute and found out that I was precluded because of what some mortals thought of me. Sure, it might be HARDER because of the weight of that mass of belief influencing Fate, but if I'm clever or persistent or whatever, shouldn't I have a chance?</div> </div> </div> </div>That depends entirely and radically on what kind of fatebond system you are working with. If it is RAW, Hera absolutely could divorce Zeus and suffer thousands of dice penalties to everything she attempts as a single woman.<br /> <br /> \u200bNot that dice penalties are very dangerous in RAW, since there is a minimum cap. But it is very annoying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2273-incendax","name":"Incendax","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T21:18:13-07:00","id":116820,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=116505#post116505\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If Hera wanted to get divorced, she would be so bound by her Fate that she couldn't use her own power to affect that? Not even Ultimate Manipulation? Even after spending 1500 years + staying away from mortals so she wouldn't have to be completely bound by their culture-wide Fatebonds?<br /> <br /> If so, that sucks for PCs who get to be gods. I would probably get a little rebellious in the game if I burned all that Legend to be an Avatar or for an Ultimate Attribute and found out that I was precluded because of what some mortals thought of me. Sure, it might be HARDER because of the weight of that mass of belief influencing Fate, but if I'm clever or persistent or whatever, shouldn't I have a chance?</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is that that selfsame power is what makes them capable of being awesome, is what limits them from breaking their own Legend. From memory (don't quote me on this), if a Scion wants to reduce their Legend, they have to make an effort to <i>not</i> use the power, and to act in such ways that are not consistent with the Legend. It's not definitive by any measure, but it certainly <i>suggests</i> that Legend and their power is closely tied.<br /> <br /> So, Hera (in the example) couldn't use Ultimate Manipulation to break Fate's hold, because Fate/Legend is what gives her that power in the first place. And if she could somehow, she'd have to rebuild her Legend to incorporate that new aspect of herself, during which she wouldn't be able to access most of her power, if she didn't lose it outright.<br /> <br /> Course, talking about how God-level powers interact at this stage is inherently problematical, since a lot of them boil down to &quot;I win&quot;, with no clear restrictions. Which is all very fine and thematic, but it does raise the problem of omnipotence, at least in part.<br /> <br /> \u200bI don't necessarily think it'd be a problem for PCs, though (the Legend-limiting bit). Assuming that these PCs have been being played for a while, their players have probably fallen into certain patterns. These patterns then form their Legend, and while they'd find it hard to break out of them in a metaphysical sense, most of the time they'd be unlikely to do so anyway because of established roles and suchforth. Plus, there's the whole 'role-playing' aspect, so as long as you discuss with your PCs what Godhood actually means, and stick to it, I wouldn't suspect it'd be an issue.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T22:49:47-07:00","id":116834,"title":"","content":"This is one of the things in Scion's cosmology that I'm not entirely on board with: the Supremacy of Fate. It is a very Eurocentric concept. In Europe we have tonnes of Prophecies and Fate deities and the general overarching belief that Fate trumps all else, as seen in the Moirai, Ragnarok, the many Prophecies of the Tuatha, the Tablet of Destinies and so forth. Outside of Europe on the other hand, things are different. The Egyptians don't seem to really care about Fate one way or another, The Orisha believe that Destiny is an inner potential to be achieved, not an outer force being forced upon you, the Deva believe Fate is an Illusion and one they have all transcended through Moksha. Explicitly making Fate the Ultimate Power That Is puts a downer on those alternate views.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T23:47:43-07:00","id":116846,"title":"","content":"It's not much to go on, but in talking about 2e, Neall's mentioned that mythology is partially driven by acts of Gods and Fate.<br /> <br /> Which tells us, among other things, that the actions the Gods take matter. Fate alone doesn't drive mythology.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-21T09:29:34-07:00","id":116989,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Allan53</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=116820#post116820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, Hera (in the example) couldn't use Ultimate Manipulation to break Fate's hold, because Fate/Legend is what gives her that power in the first place. And if she could somehow, she'd have to rebuild her Legend to incorporate that new aspect of herself, during which she wouldn't be able to access most of her power, if she didn't lose it outright.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmmm..... To me, Hera's role as goddess of marriage, fidelity, whatever you need to be the kind of person to stay with Zeus, is still manifest in the modern world explicitly in all the laws and customs that are integral to marriage. Even where the laws of marriage don't have a lineal dissent from Roman Law (which is a chunk of the world), said laws have had a huge effect on the way marriage is handled throughout a chunk of the world (pretty much anywhere Christianity has triumphed, for example, though that's not the limiting case... English Common Law holds significant sway wherever the British Empire once did, and, even though a lot of the roots are Germanic, there is a TON of Roman influence on it from multiple different directions.). So, if that's the Fate that is shaping Hera, she might already have the ability to divorce Zeus, particularly for infidelity, though in a lot of places, no more reason is required than that she wants to. <br /> <br /> So, I guess my postulation is post hoc. Hera has already been using her Ultimate Manipulation to create an escape clause in her Fatebound role for centuries by careful adjustment of public perception, supporting women's rights, etc. Perhaps even with the tacit assistance of other gods. Athena springs to mind. She might not be 100% on board with making daddy unhappy, but HER Fatebound role of being the Eidolon of Justice might influence her into Hera's sphere on this, knowingly or not. (There's that Ultimate Manipulation again... with a dash of Ultimate Charisma). <br /> <br /> The other side of this is a generalized belief that Fatebound roles aren't 100% static. I think, if your going to have a Dodekatheon that represents both the Greek and Roman conceptions of the same deities, you probably also need to have a way to have those deities have a chance to shift around in their roles a little. My big example on this would be Ares to Mars. If they are the same guy, SOMETHING happened. The petulant mama's boy/mass murdering psychopath (moreso than other gods that is) of the Iliad is not at all like the guy the Roman Legions were so fond of. Ares had to do some growing up along the way. Yes, still the God of War, but different.<br /> <br /> The only way I see around that is having Fatebound roles that can be changed based on who the god is currently around. &quot;Oh, that's the Ares side of Mars/Ares... He's not like that when he comes here. We get the guy who doesn't froth at the mouth...&quot; Even if that seems okay, then why can't Hera just use her Epic Manipulation to create a place/time/group where her role includes the idea of being divorced and staying there (or in that part of Olympus? or that wing of the palace in the uber-Dodekatheon multi-part palace (ya know, not the Mycenean throne room, the Late Roman Imperial throne room for today's audiences...) <br /> <br /> The other way it go &quot;splitter&quot; and have multiple different pantheons with very huge overlaps in associated myths. In which case my arguments fall through. Fatebound roles are so strong that the gods can never change and Hera can't divorce Zeus because she never did. She can run away for a bit, but she's always Fated to go back to him. Same with Juno, who happens to have exactly the same myth, but it's about her and Jupiter (which is just a Roman contraction of &quot;Zeus Pater&quot; anwyays). <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-21T09:36:07-07:00","id":116991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=116834#post116834\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is one of the things in Scion's cosmology that I'm not entirely on board with: the Supremacy of Fate. It is a very Eurocentric concept. In Europe we have tonnes of Prophecies and Fate deities and the general overarching belief that Fate trumps all else, as seen in the Moirai, Ragnarok, the many Prophecies of the Tuatha, the Tablet of Destinies and so forth. Outside of Europe on the other hand, things are different. The Egyptians don't seem to really care about Fate one way or another, The Orisha believe that Destiny is an inner potential to be achieved, not an outer force being forced upon you, the Deva believe Fate is an Illusion and one they have all transcended through Moksha. Explicitly making Fate the Ultimate Power That Is puts a downer on those alternate views.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed to a degree. Not as much a straightjacket as the whole &quot;this is about a modern Titanomachy&quot; thing and shoehorning that in to every mythology in the world, but Fate doesn't seem to be THAT important. A lot of the Greek gods got along fine without it's huge weight on their shoulders (sure, Zeus had one, but what about all his brothers and sisters? did they get taken down like their uncles and aunts when his pissed off son shows up?)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-21T16:39:03-07:00","id":117231,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=116834#post116834\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is one of the things in Scion's cosmology that I'm not entirely on board with: the Supremacy of Fate. It is a very Eurocentric concept. In Europe we have tonnes of Prophecies and Fate deities and the general overarching belief that Fate trumps all else, as seen in the Moirai, Ragnarok, the many Prophecies of the Tuatha, the Tablet of Destinies and so forth. Outside of Europe on the other hand, things are different. The Egyptians don't seem to really care about Fate one way or another, The Orisha believe that Destiny is an inner potential to be achieved, not an outer force being forced upon you, the Deva believe Fate is an Illusion and one they have all transcended through Moksha. Explicitly making Fate the Ultimate Power That Is puts a downer on those alternate views.</div> </div> </div> </div>I see what you're saying, but apart from the Egyptians, none of the pantheons or traditions you mention are represented in Scion (I think?), and the Egyptians don't care, so I'm kind of OK with it. Introducing them, as you point out, would involve either mangling them or putting them outside Fate, which would throw the whole cosmology sideways. So I'd say it's a fair decision, as a game-system if not theologically.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=116989#post116989\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hmmm..... To me, Hera's role as goddess of marriage, fidelity, whatever you need to be the kind of person to stay with Zeus, is still manifest in the modern world explicitly in all the laws and customs that are integral to marriage. Even where the laws of marriage don't have a lineal dissent from Roman Law (which is a chunk of the world), said laws have had a huge effect on the way marriage is handled throughout a chunk of the world (pretty much anywhere Christianity has triumphed, for example, though that's not the limiting case... English Common Law holds significant sway wherever the British Empire once did, and, even though a lot of the roots are Germanic, there is a TON of Roman influence on it from multiple different directions.). So, if that's the Fate that is shaping Hera, she might already have the ability to divorce Zeus, particularly for infidelity, though in a lot of places, no more reason is required than that she wants to.<br /> <br /> So, I guess my postulation is post hoc. Hera has already been using her Ultimate Manipulation to create an escape clause in her Fatebound role for centuries by careful adjustment of public perception, supporting women's rights, etc. Perhaps even with the tacit assistance of other gods. Athena springs to mind. She might not be 100% on board with making daddy unhappy, but HER Fatebound role of being the Eidolon of Justice might influence her into Hera's sphere on this, knowingly or not. (There's that Ultimate Manipulation again... with a dash of Ultimate Charisma).<br /> <br /> The other side of this is a generalized belief that Fatebound roles aren't 100% static. I think, if your going to have a Dodekatheon that represents both the Greek and Roman conceptions of the same deities, you probably also need to have a way to have those deities have a chance to shift around in their roles a little. My big example on this would be Ares to Mars. If they are the same guy, SOMETHING happened. The petulant mama's boy/mass murdering psychopath (moreso than other gods that is) of the Iliad is not at all like the guy the Roman Legions were so fond of. Ares had to do some growing up along the way. Yes, still the God of War, but different.<br /> <br /> The only way I see around that is having Fatebound roles that can be changed based on who the god is currently around. &quot;Oh, that's the Ares side of Mars/Ares... He's not like that when he comes here. We get the guy who doesn't froth at the mouth...&quot; Even if that seems okay, then why can't Hera just use her Epic Manipulation to create a place/time/group where her role includes the idea of being divorced and staying there (or in that part of Olympus? or that wing of the palace in the uber-Dodekatheon multi-part palace (ya know, not the Mycenean throne room, the Late Roman Imperial throne room for today's audiences...)<br /> <br /> The other way it go &quot;splitter&quot; and have multiple different pantheons with very huge overlaps in associated myths. In which case my arguments fall through. Fatebound roles are so strong that the gods can never change and Hera can't divorce Zeus because she never did. She can run away for a bit, but she's always Fated to go back to him. Same with Juno, who happens to have exactly the same myth, but it's about her and Jupiter (which is just a Roman contraction of &quot;Zeus Pater&quot; anwyays).</div> </div> </div> </div>Your point is well made. I suppose it would seem that their roles and Legend can move around a bit, but I still maintain that there's a difference between a slight change of image (maturing, in your case of Ares/Mars) and a massive break (Hera no longer being defined as Zeus' wife, which is how she's mostly known, rightly or not). It's like flexibility: people can learn to contort themselves pretty extremely, but there are limits to how we can bend without breaking, ie bones and such (don't worry about showing me videos of contortionists, I'm being figurative).<br /> <br /> Course, if they can move their Legend a little, that implies that it's not static. Which means even big bits can change, just take infinitely more effort. In the case of Hera, I suppose she'd have to build up her Legend so she has identity outside of Zeus' wife, and work to emphasise that over time, so that the other part of her identity becomes less prominent, and thus less bound by Fate/Legend/whatever. Which, as you point out, she may well have been trying to do. Which then raises an interesting question about Zeus: his philandering, is it because that's who he is, or is it to emphasise Hera's role as his long-suffering but eternally loyal wife for reasons of his own (like, maybe it's part of an alliance with another Pantheon, which may be now forgotten or whatever. Or maybe he's just a tool and likes screwing with her)?<br /> <br /> \u200bHmm, interesting notions...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-21T18:56:06-07:00","id":117307,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Allan53</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=117231#post117231\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I see what you're saying, but apart from the Egyptians, none of the pantheons or traditions you mention are represented in Scion (I think?),</div> </div> </div> </div>They do, actually. The Deva were presented as a playable Pantheon in Companion, though that write-up could...use a few changes (and I'll leave it at that). And The Orisha also exist, only in their more modern form as the Loa. From what I gather 2e will be bringing the Loa more in line with their ancient African roots, so they may well be called Orisha now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-21T22:10:45-07:00","id":117411,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=117307#post117307\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> They do, actually. The Deva were presented as a playable Pantheon in Companion, though that write-up could...use a few changes (and I'll leave it at that). And The Orisha also exist, only in their more modern form as the Loa. From what I gather 2e will be bringing the Loa more in line with their ancient African roots, so they may well be called Orisha now.</div> </div> </div> </div>And that'd be fair: new edition, new rules, albeit often similar to old rules.<br /> <br /> But anyway, they're called the Loa, and the pantheon is clearly identified as the Voodoo pantheon. Not Vodoun, or any of the other root theologies it draws from: voodoo. That implies a particular form and mythology. Islam and Christianity theologies aren't 'wrong' because they differ from Judaism and Zorastrianism, they're just different, albeit with some similarities.<br /> <br /> I'll give you Companion, I've only leafed through that. I suppose the only thing I can point out is that that's an expansion book, and thus came out later, and thus may not be consistent with previous ideas. That said, I started out gaming in D&amp;D, where 'consistency' was not a high priority for expansion books, so I may be more willing to dismiss them than the average gamer.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-21T23:06:42-07:00","id":117434,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/115776-godly-marriage-and-divorce?p=117307#post117307\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> They do, actually. The Deva were presented as a playable Pantheon in Companion, though that write-up could...use a few changes (and I'll leave it at that). And The Orisha also exist, only in their more modern form as the Loa. From what I gather 2e will be bringing the Loa more in line with their ancient African roots, so they may well be called Orisha now.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well... all we know on that front is the Loa are being heavily changed, not _how_ they're being changed. It may be that we see something more accurate to Voodoo/Vodoun instead.<br /> <br /> Joe Carriker's noted he felt he was crowbarring Guinee into an Underworld realm in 1e (Joe's responsible for the Underworld section in Demigod), which may point to him doing something more accurate in 2e.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}